.TH KARIDNS\-ZONE 8 "2026-11-13" "KariDNS" "KariDNS Manual"
.\" Generated from docs/karidns-zone.md by man/md2man.pl (make man): edit the Markdown manual, not this page.
.SH NAME
karidns\-zone \- writes a zone file into a PowerDNS 5.1 database (\fBimport\fR)
and a zone of the database into a zone file (\fBexport\fR)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.RS 2
.nf
karidns\-zone import [options] <zone> <file|\->
karidns\-zone import \-\-all [options] <tinydns\-data\-file>
karidns\-zone import \-\-signer [options] <zone> <signed\-zone\-file|\->
karidns\-zone export [options] <zone> [<file>]
karidns\-zone export \-\-signer [options] <zone> [<file>]
karidns\-zone export \-\-all \-\-dir <dir> [options]
karidns\-zone apply [options] \-\-changes <file|\-> <zone>
karidns\-zone apply [options] \-\-zone\-file <file|\-> [\-\-delete\-others] <zone>
karidns\-zone \-\-types\-list [\-\-json]
karidns\-zone render \-\-json <zone>
karidns\-zone \-\-version | \-h | \-\-help
.fi
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBkaridns\-zone\fR is the command line of the 0.6.0 ZoneImporter and
ZoneExporter. It reads the settings of the database the way
\fBkaridns\-dbloader(8)\fR and \fBkaricheck db\fR do (the \fBdatabase { }\fR
block and the views of \fBkaridns.conf\fR), or takes them from the command
line. It is an ordinary program: it runs as an ordinary user and never needs
root. The server and the loader do not use it; it is how zone files get into the
database and out of it again (backups, the DB-outage fallback below, a signer
that writes its records through \fB\-\-types\fR), and, with \fBapply\fR, how the
WebAdmin API changes the RRsets of a zone (below).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBimport\fR reads a zone file (BIND format, with \fB$INCLUDE\fR,
\fB$GENERATE\fR, \fB$LOCATION\fR and \fB$ECS\-SUBNET\fR tags; or a tinydns data
file) with the parser of the server and writes the zone as rows of the PowerDNS
schema, in ONE transaction per zone: owners in lower case without the final dot,
MX and SRV priorities in \fBprio\fR, a signed zone in the layout PowerDNS stores
for a zone with \fBPRESIGNED=1\fR (see \fBkaridns(8)\fR, "Database zones"), tags
and tinydns attributes in the \fBkaridns_*\fR tables. It does not run SQL text:
every value is a bound parameter.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBapply\fR changes RRsets of a zone from a change file, with the zone's
generation checked, the serial raised, the version of the change written and a
JSON diff of the RRsets (below, "apply and the change file").
.IP \(bu 2
\fBexport\fR writes a zone of the database as a zone file. The zone is read the
way the loader reads it (every row checked, a presigned zone with the NSEC3PARAM
/ NSEC3 / NSEC records the server rebuilds), so what is exported is what the
server serves. The text is in \fBcanonical order\fR (RFC 4034 section 6.1, owner
names first, then type, then RDATA), so two exports of the same zone are the
same file and \fBdiff\fR shows the real change. The text is parsed again before
a byte is written and must give the same records.
.SS What an import does
.IP 1. 4
Reads the text (a file, \fB\-\fR for standard input, at most 64 MiB there) and
parses it. A syntax error names the file and the line.
.IP 2. 4
Runs the checks of \fBkaricheck zone\fR on it (with the \fBcheck\-*\fR options
of the configuration, below). An \fBerror\fR fails the import and nothing is
written. A \fBwarning\fR is printed and the import goes on; \fB\-\-strict\fR
makes a warning fail it.
.IP 3. 4
In one transaction: finds the zone or creates it, applies the serial rule,
deletes and inserts the rows, puts the zone in the view, adds the reload
request.
.IP 4. 4
\fBReads the zone back in the same transaction\fR through the loader's reader
(\fBdns_pdns_reader.c\fR), applies the size limits of the configuration and the
checks of \fBkaricheck db\fR to it, and commits \fBonly if the loader would load
what was written.\fR A merge that puts a CNAME beside other data, an ALIAS
beside an A record, a zone over \fBmax\-records\fR, rows the reader refuses: the
transaction is rolled back and the database is as it was
(\fBdb.import.verify\fR, \fBzone.limits\fR, \fBzone.cname.coexists\fR, ...). A
dry run does all of this too.
.IP 5. 4
Commits. The triggers of \fBdb/karidns_ext.*.sql\fR write the change to
\fBkaridns_changes\fR, so a running server with \fBauto\-reload\fR picks the
zone up by itself; \fB\-\-reload\fR also adds a row to
\fBkaridns_reload_requests\fR (PostgreSQL), and otherwise \fBkarictl reload\fR
does it.
.PP
The type of an existing zone (\fBdomains.type\fR) is never changed; a new zone
is \fBNATIVE\fR (what \fBpdnsutil load\-zone\fR creates), or \fBMASTER\fR with
\fB\-\-type MASTER\fR. A zone whose \fBdomains.type\fR is not \fBNATIVE\fR or
\fBMASTER\fR is not a zone the server serves from records: the import goes on,
with the warning \fBdb.import.not\-primary\fR, and the records are not read
back.
.SS Modes
.TP 8
\fB\-\-mode replace\fR (the default)
The records of the zone are replaced by the file's. The metadata the importer
owns (\fBPRESIGNED\fR, \fBNSEC3PARAM\fR, \fBX\-KARIDNS\-FORMAT\fR) and the
zone's tag definitions (\fBkaridns_zone_tagdefs\fR) are replaced too; every
other \fBdomainmetadata\fR row of the zone (\fBALLOW\-AXFR\-FROM\fR, profile
names, ...) is kept.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-mode merge\fR
\fBRRset merge.\fR Every (owner, type) in the file replaces that RRset in the
database; the RRsets that are not in the file stay. Deleting needs replace
mode. The file may be a part of the zone: the checks of the file do not demand
SOA, NS and glue then (the whole zone is checked when it is read back). The
owned metadata the file declares replaces the database's.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-types T1,T2\fR
Limits either mode to records of these types: only they are deleted and only
they are written; metadata and tag definitions are not touched. (The signer does
not use it: it has \fB\-\-signer\fR, below.) Up to 64 types, case does not
matter.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR (\fB\-n\fR)
Does everything except the commit. The RRsets that would be added, replaced or
deleted are listed (\fBdb.import.change\fR, notes), and the summary says \fBdry
run, nothing was written\fR. \fB\-\-verbose\fR lists them for a real import too.
.SS SOA serial (RFC 1982)
For an existing zone whose SOA is written, the new serial must be \fBgreater\fR
than the serial in the database (serial number arithmetic, RFC 1982 section
3.2); otherwise the import fails with \fBdb.import.serial\fR. Secondaries and
IXFR only see a change when the serial grows.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-serial grow\fR (the default)
The rule above.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-serial increment\fR
Writes the database serial + 1, whatever the file says.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-serial keep\fR
Writes the file's serial as it is (restoring a backup, importing an export of
the same zone).
.PP
A new zone takes the file's serial. A merge or a \fB\-\-types\fR import that
does not write the SOA does not touch the serial.
.SS Signer mode (\-\-signer)
\fBkaridns\-zone import \-\-signer <zone> <file>\fR writes a zone that
\fBkaridns\-signer\fR signed (0.6.0 item C). The file is the zone as
\fBdnssec\-signzone\fR wrote it from an \fBexport\fR of the zone; the tool takes
from it the DNSSEC records and nothing else, so it works with the rights of the
signer role alone (below) and cannot change the data of the zone. In ONE
transaction:
.IP 1. 4
The \fBRRSIG\fR, \fBNSEC\fR, \fBNSEC3\fR, \fBNSEC3PARAM\fR and \fBDNSKEY\fR
records of the database are replaced by the file's, in the layout PowerDNS
stores a presigned zone in (no \fBNSEC\fR, \fBNSEC3\fR or \fBNSEC3PARAM\fR rows,
the chain is rebuilt by the reader; \fBordername\fR is the NSEC3 hash, or the
reversed relative name of an NSEC zone). \fBCDS\fR and \fBCDNSKEY\fR are not
touched. \fBOnly the rows that differ are written\fR (0.6.1): both sides are
sorted by owner, type, RDATA text, TTL, priority, \fBordername\fR and \fBauth\fR
and walked once; a row the database has and the file has too (field for field,
the text of the RDATA as it is spelled) stays untouched, with its id, a row only
in the database is deleted (a disabled one too), a row only in the file is
inserted. A signing that changes one record therefore writes a few rows, and the
change log (SQLite3: a row per changed row) and the audit log list those. The
metadata of step 5 is written only where it differs, too. A row spelled
differently from the file's is replaced, never kept wrongly.
.IP 2. 4
The SOA of the file must be the database's SOA but for the serial, which must be
\fBgreater\fR (RFC 1982); it is written with \fBUPDATE\fR.
.IP 3. 4
\fBEvery other record of the file must equal the database's\fR, compared as
RRsets in canonical form (RFC 4034 section 6.2): owner (without regard to case),
type, class, the RDATA in wire form with the names inside the RDATA of the types
that section lists in lower case, and the TTL of the RRset (the lowest of its
records, RFC 2181 section 5.2: the TTL the server serves). Both sides are read
the way the server reads a zone (the zone parser for the file, the loader's
reader for the rows; the DNSSEC records left out), so the way a row is spelled
does not matter: hex or names in upper case, an AAAA that is not compressed, a
TXT without quotes or with escapes, the same row twice, an RRset whose rows have
different TTLs, a NULL TTL (the default TTL of the loader), a disabled row (not
served, not compared). An export of a zone, signed and given back, is accepted
whatever the rows look like; a real change is not. If the zone changed since the
export, nothing is written: the message is \fBdb.import.changed\fR (it names the
first RRset that differs), the \fB\-\-json\fR document has \fB"signer":
{"changed": true}\fR and the exit status is \fB3\fR. The signer then signs the
zone again. The same goes for a serial that does not grow, and for a change that
another session commits while the tool writes: on PostgreSQL the transaction is
\fBREPEATABLE READ\fR, so a row it writes that another session changed after the
tool read the zone (SQLSTATE 40001, or a record that is gone) is a change of the
zone, not a failure; on SQLite3 the one write lock rules it out.
.IP 4. 4
\fBordername\fR and \fBauth\fR of the records that stay (and of the empty
non-terminals the database holds) are set as a presigned zone has them
(\fBauth\fR 0 for the NS records of a delegation and the names below it), with
\fBUPDATE\fR where they differ.
.IP 5. 4
The metadata \fBPRESIGNED\fR = 1, \fBNSEC3PARAM\fR (none for an NSEC zone) and
\fBX\-KARIDNS\-SIGNER\fR = 1 are written (where they are not so already). A
running server then no longer reloads the zone because of the change log, only
on a reload request (\fBkaridns(8)\fR, \fBsigner\-wait\fR).
.IP 6. 4
The zone is read back through the loader's reader as for any import; a zone the
loader would refuse is not committed.
.PP
\fB\-\-signer\fR takes a zone that exists, is a primary zone (\fBNATIVE\fR or
\fBMASTER\fR) and has one SOA at the apex, and a file in BIND format that is
signed (RRSIG records and a complete NSEC or NSEC3 chain; otherwise
\fBdb.import.signer\fR). It cannot be combined with \fB\-\-mode\fR,
\fB\-\-types\fR, \fB\-\-serial\fR, \fB\-\-view\fR, \fB\-\-type\fR, \fB\-\-all\fR
or a tinydns data file: those are usage errors (exit status
2). \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR, \fB\-\-reload\fR (PostgreSQL: the reload request is
committed with the data, so the loader sees both together), \fB\-\-strict\fR,
\fB\-\-verbose\fR, \fB\-\-audit\fR and \fB\-\-json\fR work as for any import. A
signer import writes a whole zone again (every signature), so on PostgreSQL it
asks for the audit in \fBsummary mode\fR unless \fB\-\-audit rows\fR is given,
and it writes ordername and auth, the DNSSEC rows and the metadata in a few
statements (\fBunnest()\fR of arrays): the change log gets a few rows and
\fBkaridns_changes\fR one \fBNOTIFY\fR per statement, not one per
record. (SQLite3 triggers fire per row whatever the statements: the change log
and the audit have a row per changed row.)
.PP
The first signing needs nothing in the database but the zone: no
\fBcryptokeys\fR rows and no \fBPRESIGNED\fR metadata. \fBkaridns\-zone
export\fR writes the zone as the unsigned zone it is, and \fBimport
\-\-signer\fR of the signed text then sets \fBPRESIGNED\fR, \fBNSEC3PARAM\fR
(NSEC3) and \fBX\-KARIDNS\-SIGNER\fR; the signer inserts the keys it signed with
after that.
.PP
\fBA normal import into a zone the signer signs.\fR A zone with the metadata
\fBX\-KARIDNS\-SIGNER\fR = 1 keeps its DNSSEC records when an operator imports
into it with a plain \fBkaridns\-zone import\fR (replace or merge): the
\fBRRSIG\fR, \fBNSEC\fR, \fBNSEC3\fR, \fBNSEC3PARAM\fR, \fBDNSKEY\fR, \fBCDS\fR
and \fBCDNSKEY\fR rows and the metadata \fBPRESIGNED\fR and \fBNSEC3PARAM\fR
stay as they are, and only the other data is replaced (or merged); a file with
DNSSEC records of its own is taken without them (the message
\fBdb.import.signer\-zone\fR says how many were ignored). The read-back checks
the zone without its DNSSEC records. The loader does not serve the new data
before the signer has signed it (\fBsigner\-wait\fR); the signer notices the
change in the change log and signs the zone again.
.PP
\fBResult.\fR The text output ends with \fBZone 'x' signed: SOA serial A \-> B,
N DNSSEC record(s) written, M removed, K record(s) updated (ordername, auth);
the earliest RRSIG expires T\fR. With \fB\-\-json\fR the document has one more
key, \fBsigner\fR, after \fBzones\fR:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
"signer": { "zone": "example.test", "changed": false, "committed": true, "serial": 11, "old_serial": 10,
            "dnssec_records_written": 20, "dnssec_records_removed": 0, "records_updated": 9,
            "rrsets_added": 16, "rrsets_replaced": 0, "rrsets_deleted": 0,
            "earliest_expire": "2026\-11\-20T02:29:12Z", "earliest_expire_epoch": 1795141752,
            "write_id": { "xid": null, "seq_first": 11, "seq_last": 42 } }
.fi
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fBearliest_expire\fR is the earliest signature expiration of the zone
\fBafter\fR the write, as a UTC time (and \fBearliest_expire_epoch\fR, unix
seconds). The expiration field of an RRSIG is a 32-bit number of seconds modulo
2^32, compared with serial number arithmetic (RFC 4034 section 3.1.5, RFC 1982),
so the earliest is the one that comes first in that order, and the 32-bit value
is put into the 64-bit time nearest to now. The signer schedules the next run
from it: the earliest expiration minus its refresh interval.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBwrite_id\fR says which rows of \fBkaridns_changes\fR this write made, so that
the signer does not take its own write for a change of the zone. PostgreSQL:
\fBxid\fR is \fBpg_current_xact_id()\fR of the write transaction (the \fBxid\fR
column of the rows the triggers wrote), \fBseq_first\fR and \fBseq_last\fR are
null. SQLite3: \fBseq_first\fR and \fBseq_last\fR are the first and the last
\fBkaridns_changes.seq\fR of the transaction (the transaction holds the one
write lock, so all rows between them are its own; both null when it wrote no
row, and \fBxid\fR is null).
.IP \(bu 2
With \fBchanged\fR true there is no other key but \fBzone\fR and \fBcommitted\fR
(false). A dry run gives the same numbers with \fBcommitted\fR false.
.PP
\fBBusy.\fR When another importer holds the zone, or the database is locked for
longer than the tool waits (SQLite3: 5 seconds; PostgreSQL: \fBlock_timeout\fR,
10 seconds), nothing is written and the exit status is \fB4\fR
(\fBdb.import.busy\fR; \fB"signer": {"changed": false, "committed": false,
"busy": true}\fR with \fB\-\-json\fR), not 1: try again. Any other import exits
with 1 in this case.
.SS Views and the database
The database comes from, in this order: \fB\-\-sqlite3 FILE\fR or
\fB\-\-conninfo\-file FILE\fR (with \fB\-\-schema NAME\fR), else the
configuration. \fB\-c FILE\fR names the configuration; without it
\fB/usr/local/etc/karidns/karidns.conf\fR (or \fB/usr/local/etc/karidns.conf\fR)
is read when it exists. \fB\-\-no\-config\fR reads none. With a configuration:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBoptions { database { driver ...; database\-file ...; } }\fR (or
\fBconninfo\-file\fR, \fBschema\fR) is the database; \fB\-\-view NAME\fR selects
a view of the configuration: its \fBdatabase\-file\fR / \fBdatabase\-schema\fR
if it has one of its own, else the shared source. A view with \fBdatabase
none;\fR takes no zones (\fBdb.view\-none\fR); a view that is not defined is
\fBdb.view\-not\-found\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
With \fB\-\-view\fR and the shared source, \fBimport\fR puts the zone in that
view (\fBkaridns_domain_views\fR; the loader serves a zone without any row in
every view) and \fBexport\fR lists only the zones of that view. A view with a
source of its own ignores \fBkaridns_domain_views\fR: no row is written.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBThe server's rules for a database zone of that view are applied, so a zone
the server would refuse is not written:\fR the \fBcheck\-*\fR options
(\fBcheck\-names\fR, \fBcheck\-mx\fR, \fBcheck\-integrity\fR,
\fBcheck\-wildcard\fR, ... as \fBkaricheck\fR and \fBkaridns\-dbloader
\-\-check\fR use them), the size limits \fBmax\-records\fR,
\fBmax\-records\-per\-type\fR, \fBmax\-types\-per\-name\fR and
\fBmax\-zone\-ttl\fR (options, view), the location and ECS tag definitions of
\fBoptions { }\fR, and \fBdefault\-ttl\fR, \fBmax\-zone\-records\fR and
\fBmax\-zone\-bytes\fR of the database block (for the reader that reads the zone
back, and for \fBexport\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBThe file zones of the configuration\fR (\fBzone "x" { type master; file ...;
};\fR of the view, or of every view without \fB\-\-view\fR) are passed to the
importer: importing a zone that is also a file zone prints
\fBdb.import.file\-zone\fR: the file zone has priority, so the server keeps
serving the file, not the database zone (see \fBkaridns(8)\fR, D-PRIO).
.PP
Without a configuration the checks have their default severities and there are
no limits.
.SS Formats
.TP 8
\fB\-\-format bind|tinydns|auto\fR (import)
The format of the input. \fBauto\fR (the default) takes a tinydns data file for
a name ending in \fB.tinydns\fR or \fB.data\fR or called \fBdata\fR, and BIND
format for everything else, including standard input. \fB\-\-all\fR always reads
a tinydns data file. A tinydns zone is imported as a zone with
\fBX\-KARIDNS\-FORMAT tinydns\fR; the time stamps, countdown TTLs and location
codes go to \fBkaridns_record_tinydns\fR, the \fB%\fR lines to
\fBkaridns_zone_tagdefs\fR. A BIND-format file cannot hold those.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-format bind|tinydns|auto\fR (export)
\fBauto\fR (the default) writes a zone with \fBX\-KARIDNS\-FORMAT tinydns\fR as
a tinydns data file and every other zone in BIND format. \fB\-\-format bind\fR
of a tinydns zone fails and names the record when it has an attribute BIND
format cannot say. Tags and tag definitions are written as \fB$LOCATION\fR,
\fB$ECS\-SUBNET\fR and \fB$LOCATION\-TAG\fR / \fB$ECS\-SUBNET\-TAG\fR, so an
import of the export gives the same rows.
.PP
A signed (PRESIGNED) zone is exported as the server serves it, with the
NSEC3PARAM / NSEC3 / NSEC records the server rebuilds from the rows, so \fBthe
file alone can serve the zone\fR (below).
.SS apply and the change file (0.6.0 items D and E)
\fBkaridns\-zone apply \-\-changes <file|\-> <zone>\fR changes RRsets of a zone
that is in the database: the \fBRRset merge\fR of \fBimport \-\-mode merge\fR,
with deletes, from a small change file instead of a zone file. It is what the
WebAdmin API runs for every change of a zone (the API renders the file from a
request; an operator may write one by hand), and it does the work in one
transaction:
.IP 1. 4
Reads the change file (a file, \fB\-\fR for standard input, at most 64 MiB) with
the parser of \fBdns_zone_changes.c\fR (below). A line it refuses fails the
whole file, names the line and writes nothing.
.IP 2. 4
Turns the \fBrr\fR lines into zone text under \fB$ORIGIN <zone>.\fR and runs the
checks of \fBkaricheck zone\fR on it exactly as \fBimport \-\-mode merge\fR does
(a part of a zone is not judged by the SOA, NS and glue it lacks). An error
fails the change.
.IP 3. 4
In ONE transaction (\fBREPEATABLE READ\fR on PostgreSQL): locks the zone, reads
its \fBgeneration\fR (below) and fails with exit status \fB3\fR when
\fB\-\-expect\-generation\fR names another, deletes and inserts the RRsets,
\fBraises the SOA serial by one\fR when the change set changed the zone and
wrote no SOA record of its own (RFC 1982 section 3.1; with \fB\-\-principal\fR
through \fBkaridns_auth.bump_serial()\fR, the only way the API's role may change
an SOA), reads the zone back through the loader's reader (the whole zone, or
with \fB\-\-readback changed\fR only the rows written), writes the \fBversion\fR
(below) and commits, or rolls back for \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR.
.IP 4. 4
Reports the RRsets that changed, each with its records before and after, in the
text output as \fBdb.import.change\fR notes and in the \fB\-\-json\fR document
as the key \fBapply\fR (below).
.PP
A change set that changes nothing writes nothing: no version, no new serial. The
RRsets of the change that equal the zone's are not deleted and written again
either: only the RRsets that change are written (a write that changes nothing is
still a write for the audit log and for row level security). \fB\-\-zone\-file
FILE\fR (below, "OPTIONS") takes a zone file instead of a change file. A zone
that is not a primary zone (\fBSLAVE\fR, \fBCONSUMER\fR, \fBPRODUCER\fR), a zone
that is not in the database (without \fB\-\-create\fR), the deletion of the apex
SOA, and a zone that already exists (with \fB\-\-create\fR) are errors
(\fBdb.apply.zone\fR). \fB\-\-create\fR makes a new zone (\fB\-\-type NATIVE\fR
or \fBMASTER\fR, \fB\-\-view\fR): the \fBrr\fR lines are the whole zone and are
checked as one (SOA, NS and glue are needed); its version 1 has the kind
\fBcreate\fR.
.PP
\fBThe change file\fR (\fBdns_zone_changes.h\fR) has one operation per line,
fields separated by blanks, lines ended by LF (a CR before it is dropped); empty
lines and lines that begin with \fB#\fR are ignored:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
rr <owner> <ttl> <type> <rdata>
delete <owner> <type>
.fi
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrr\fR puts a record into the new RRset (owner, type): \fBevery RRset that has
\fBrr\fB lines is replaced\fR by its \fBrr\fR lines, in one step, whatever it
had before. \fBdelete\fR deletes the RRset. The same RRset in a \fBdelete\fR
line and in an \fBrr\fR line is an error; two \fBdelete\fR lines of one RRset
are one.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBowner\fR is an absolute name with the final dot, made of letters, digits,
\fB_\fR, \fB*\fR, \fB\-\fR, \fB/\fR and \fB.\fR (labels of 1 to 63 octets, 253
octets in all; case does not matter, it is stored in lower case), and it is the
zone or a name below it at a label boundary: \fBwww.example.test.\fR is in
\fBexample.test.\fR, \fBwwwexample.test.\fR is not. A name outside the zone is
an error (it is not dropped as a zone file's would be).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBttl\fR is decimal, 0 to 2147483647 (RFC 2181 section 8). \fBtype\fR is a
record type name or \fBTYPEnnn\fR, any case, letters, digits and \fB\-\fR. The
types the signer owns (\fBRRSIG NSEC NSEC3 NSEC3PARAM DNSKEY CDS CDNSKEY\fR) and
the meta types (\fBANY AXFR IXFR OPT TSIG TKEY MAILA MAILB\fR) are refused, also
in their \fBTYPEnnn\fR spelling. \fBkaridns\-zone \-\-types\-list\fR prints the
names a change file may use.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrdata\fR is the rest of the line in the presentation format of the zone
parser (names in it that are not absolute are relative to the zone), not empty,
at most 65535 octets. It must be safe in a zone file by the rules of the
loader's reader (\fBdns_pdns_reader.h\fR, RFC 1035 section 5.1): no control
character but TAB, no \fB;\fR outside quotes, no leading \fB$\fR, no open quote
or group, no trailing backslash, no bare \fB@\fR. A field can therefore never
end its line, start a directive or hide the rest of its line, whatever a
renderer put into it. The parser is fuzzed
(\fBtests/fuzz/fuzz_zone_changes.c\fR).
.PP
\fBThe generation.\fR \fBkaridns_zone_state\fR has a number per zone that grows
whenever a transaction changes a row of the zone (records, metadata, the
\fBkaridns_*\fR zone tables), written by triggers of the owner: nothing else
writes it. \fBapply \-\-dry\-run\fR prints it; \fB\-\-expect\-generation N\fR
makes the transaction fail with exit status 3 (\fBdb.import.changed\fR, nothing
written) unless the zone has that generation, and on PostgreSQL a writer that
committed after the transaction's snapshot makes its own write fail with a
serialization failure, which is the same exit status 3. So the API validates a
change set against the zone it read, and commits only when nobody changed the
zone in between (it repeats the check when it is told "changed"). Every writer
grows the number, the signer included: the SOA serial a re-sign raises is a
change of the zone, so a signed zone can make the commit run of the API exit
with 3 now and then, and the API repeats the two steps. PostgreSQL grows it once
per transaction and zone, SQLite3 once per changed row; only equality means
anything.
.PP
\fBVersions.\fR \fBkaridns_zone_versions\fR and
\fBkaridns_zone_version_rrsets\fR (db/karidns_ext.*.sql) hold one row per
committed change set of a zone: its number (per zone, from 1), the kind
(\fBcreate\fR, \fBedit\fR, \fBtemplate\fR, \fBimport\fR, \fBrollback\fR;
\fB\-\-version\-kind\fR), the principal (\fB\-\-principal\fR,
\fB\-\-principal\-name\fR) and the database role, the change set
(\fB\-\-change\-set\fR), the comment (\fB\-\-comment\fR), the SOA serial and the
generation before and after, the version a rollback restores
(\fB\-\-rollback\-of\fR), and for each RRset the change made, the RRset before
and after as canonical lines \fB<ttl> <TYPE> <rdata>\fR (names absolute, in the
order of the exporter), NULL for absent. The rows are written in the transaction
of the change itself, from the RRsets it replaced and wrote; the audit log stays
the complete record. The newest 100 versions of a zone are kept (the number in
the trigger; \fBtests/run_karidns_zone_apply_test.sh\fR replaces it by 3). The
DNSSEC types, tags and tinydns attributes, and a change of the SOA serial alone,
are not versioned. A change of more than 10000 RRsets is listed without its
RRsets (\fBdelta_complete\fR false). \fBimport\fR writes a version too (kind
\fBimport\fR, no principal) when the tables exist, so the history has no silent
gaps; a signer import (\fB\-\-signer\fR) writes none. A database without the
tables works as before.
.PP
\fBRoles.\fR An operator runs \fBapply\fR with the writer role. The WebAdmin API
runs it twice per change set: the \fBcheck run\fR (\fBapply \-\-dry\-run\fR,
then nothing stays) as the \fBcheck role\fR of
\fBdb/karidns_check_role.pgsql.sql\fR, a trusted session that sees the whole
zone and so can judge a change of a subdomain master against it, and the
\fBcommit run\fR as the API's role (\fB\-\-readback changed
\-\-expect\-generation G \-\-principal P \-\-change\-set C\fR), where row level
security checks every row again. The check role cannot commit: a transaction of
its login role that wrote a row of any table the dry run writes is refused at
COMMIT ("the check role never commits"; a deferred constraint trigger,
\fBkaridns_auth.enable_never_commit()\fR). \fB\-\-readback changed\fR reads back
only the rows this transaction wrote and runs them through the loader's reader;
the checks of the whole zone were made by the check run at the same generation
(row level security shows the API's session only the part of the zone its
principal has a grant on, so a read-back of the whole zone would judge a part),
and the tool checks that every row written is read back.
.PP
\fBThe \fBapply\fB document.\fR With \fB\-\-json\fR the document has the key
\fB"command": "apply"\fR and, after \fBzones\fR, the key \fBapply\fR:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
"apply": { "zone": "example.test.", "dry_run": true, "committed": false, "changed": false, "busy": false, "created": false,
           "generation": 17, "generation_after": 21, "serial": { "before": 41, "after": 42 }, "serial_bumped": true,
           "version": 5, "delta_complete": true, "records_before": 1200, "records_after": 1201, "warnings": 0,
           "diff": [ { "owner": "www.example.test.", "type": "A", "change": "replace",
                       "before": [ "300 A 192.0.2.1" ], "after": [ "300 A 192.0.2.10", "300 A 192.0.2.11" ] } ] }
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBdiff\fR has one entry per RRset changed, in owner, type order (the SOA RRset
too, when the file wrote it); \fBchange\fR is \fBadd\fR, \fBreplace\fR or
\fBdelete\fR; \fBbefore\fR / \fBafter\fR are arrays of the canonical lines, null
when the RRset was not / is not there. \fBversion\fR is the number the version
row has (a dry run writes it and rolls it back); \fBrecords_before\fR /
\fBrecords_after\fR count the records the session could see. When the zone
changed (exit status 3) or is busy (4) the document has \fBchanged\fR or
\fBbusy\fR true and no other key but \fBzone\fR, \fBdry_run\fR, \fBcommitted\fR
and \fBcreated\fR. The messages (the zone checks) are in \fBmessages[]\fR as
always, with the codes of \fBkaricheck(8)\fR.
.PP
\fB\fB\-\-types\-list\fB\fR prints the record types a change file may name, one
per line (with \fB\-\-json\fR: the key \fBtypes\fR, \fB"command": "types"\fR).
.SS render (0.6.0 item F, the WebAdmin GUI)
\fBkaridns\-zone render \-\-json <zone>\fR turns \fBstored PowerDNS records\fR
into the text a change file takes as \fBrdata\fR. It reads no database and no
configuration: it is a filter, and it is what the WebAdmin API runs (one run for
a page of RRsets) for \fBGET .../rrsets?rdata=1\fR, so that what a client read
can be sent back in a change set unchanged. The stored form
(\fBrecords.content\fR) is not that form: names have no final dot (the change
file reads them relative to the zone), and the priority of MX and SRV is in a
column of its own.
.PP
Standard input has one record a line, four fields separated by one TAB, lines
ended by LF (the last may lack it):
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
<owner> TAB <type> TAB <prio> TAB <content>
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBowner\fR and \fBtype\fR are \fBrecords.name\fR and \fBrecords.type\fR,
\fBprio\fR is \fBrecords.prio\fR as decimal digits (an optional \fB\-\fR sign)
or \fB\-\fR for NULL, \fBcontent\fR is \fBrecords.content\fR, the rest of the
line (it may hold TABs). A line that does not have this shape, or more than
200,000 lines, refuses the whole input (exit status 1, \fBdb.render.input\fR,
nothing on standard output but the document); it is the caller's mistake. A
record that the loader's reader refuses is not: each record is judged alone.
.PP
Each record is read as the loader reads a row (\fBdns_pdns_reader.c\fR: the
line-safety rules of RFC 1035 section 5.1, the priority of MX and SRV put in
front, the zone parser), inside a zone of its own for \fB<zone>\fR, and written
by the zone-file writer of the exporter (\fBdns_zone_text.c\fR: every name
absolute, the root name as \fB.\fR, strings quoted and escaped, RFC 3597 generic
form for the types that have none). The text is given only if it \fBreads back
as the same record\fR: it is read again, as a record with the priority in its
content (the way a change set brings it back), and must be the same type and the
same canonical RDATA (RFC 4034 section 6.2). There is no second RDATA
parser. PowerDNS \fBALIAS\fR and \fBLUA\fR have no text of their own and are
refused (shown as stored).
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
"render": { "rdata": [ "10 mail2.example.test.", null, "\e"hello\e" \e"wor ld\e"" ],
            "errors": [ { "index": 1, "text": "invalid IPv4 address literal in A record" } ] }
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBrdata\fR has one entry for each input line, in order: the text, or
\fBnull\fR; \fBerrors\fR says why for each \fBnull\fR (the reader's reason
without the zone or the row; \fBindex\fR counts from 0). The exit status is 0
when the input was understood, whatever became of the records. \fB\-\-json\fR is
required, and no other option is (the answer is a document of the schema of
\fBkaricheck \-\-json\fR, \fB"command": "render"\fR). The input path is fuzzed
(\fBtests/fuzz/fuzz_zone_changes.c\fR, which also checks that a rendered text is
one safe line that renders again, to a fixed point).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP 8
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-config FILE\fR
The configuration file (above).
.TP 8
\fB\-\-no\-config\fR
Read no configuration.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-view NAME\fR
The view (above).
.TP 8
\fB\-\-sqlite3 FILE\fR
The SQLite3 database file. The importer needs read and write permission on the
file and its directory (the database is in WAL mode).
.TP 8
\fB\-\-conninfo\-file FILE\fR, \fB\-\-schema NAME\fR
PostgreSQL: the file with the libpq connection string (a regular file owned by
the user running the tool, mode 0600 or 0400; checked before it is read), and
the schema that holds the tables (default: the \fBsearch_path\fR of the role).
.TP 8
\fB\-\-all\fR
\fBimport\fR: every zone with an SOA (\fB.\fR or \fBZ\fR line) of a tinydns data
file, each in a transaction of its own; a zone that fails does not stop the
others. \fBexport\fR: every primary zone of the database (needs \fB\-\-dir\fR).
.TP 8
\fB\-\-mode replace|merge\fR, \fB\-\-types T1,T2\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR (\fB\-n\fR), \fB\-\-serial grow|increment|keep\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Above.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-signer\fR
\fBimport\fR: the file is a zone signed by \fBkaridns\-signer\fR; write its
DNSSEC records and nothing else (above). Exit status 3 when the zone changed
since the file was made, 4 when the zone is busy. The audit is in summary mode
unless \fB\-\-audit rows\fR is given.
.RS 8
.PP
\fBexport\fR: the zone as the input of \fBdnssec\-signzone\fR (below, "Export
for the signer"): the NSEC / NSEC3 chain (rows the PowerDNS layout does not
have) and the metadata \fBPRESIGNED\fR and \fBNSEC3PARAM\fR are left out, the
signatures (those over the chain too, since 0.6.1) and the DNSKEY records stay.
.RE
.TP 8
\fB\-\-strict\fR
A warning of the zone checks fails the import. Without it warnings are printed
and the import goes on. An error always fails.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-reload\fR
Also add a row to \fBkaridns_reload_requests\fR in the transaction (PostgreSQL),
so every server on the database reloads the zone. A database without the table
gives the warning \fBdb.import.no\-reload\fR.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-type NATIVE|MASTER\fR
\fBdomains.type\fR of a \fBnew\fR zone (default \fBNATIVE\fR).
.TP 8
\fB\-\-audit rows|summary\fR
\fBimport\fR: how the audit log sees this import (default \fBrows\fR; see AUDIT
below). Any other value is a usage error, and so is the option with
\fBexport\fR.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-format bind|tinydns|auto\fR
Above.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-dir DIR\fR
\fBexport \-\-all\fR: the directory for one file per zone; made when it does not
exist (one level).
.TP 8
\fB\-\-force\fR
\fBexport\fR: replace output files that exist. Without it an existing file is an
error and is not touched.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-json\fR
One JSON document on standard output (below).
.TP 8
\fB\-\-changes FILE\fR (apply)
The change file; \fB\-\fR is standard input. Required, unless
\fB\-\-zone\-file\fR is given.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-zone\-file FILE\fR (apply)
Instead of \fB\-\-changes\fR: the RRsets of a \fBzone file\fR (BIND format;
\fB\-\fR is standard input, where \fB$INCLUDE\fR is refused; the plain records
only, no DNSSEC records, location / ECS tags or tinydns attributes) replace
those RRsets of the zone, as \fBimport \-\-mode merge\fR does, with the checks,
the generation, the version and the read-back of \fBapply\fR. An apex SOA of the
file that differs from the zone's in the serial only is left alone (the serial
is raised as for any change, \fB\-\-serial increment\fR); one that differs
otherwise is written. It is what the WebAdmin API runs for an import.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-delete\-others\fR (apply)
With \fB\-\-zone\-file\fR: also delete the RRsets of the zone that the file does
not have, except the apex SOA and, in a zone kept signed by
\fBkaridns\-signer\fR, the DNSSEC records. Not with \fB\-\-create\fR.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-create\fR (apply)
The zone must not exist and is made; the \fBrr\fR lines (or the zone file) are
the whole zone (\fB\-\-type NATIVE|MASTER\fR, \fB\-\-view\fR).
.TP 8
\fB\-\-expect\-generation N\fR (apply)
Fail with exit status 3 unless the zone has this generation. Not with
\fB\-\-create\fR.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-serial grow|increment|keep\fR (apply)
As for import, but the default is \fBincrement\fR: a change that wrote no SOA of
its own raises the serial by one; an SOA in the change file gets the database
serial + 1.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-principal ID\fR, \fB\-\-principal\-name NAME\fR, \fB\-\-change\-set ID\fR (apply)
Who acts and for which change set. PostgreSQL: \fBSET LOCAL karidns.principal\fR
and \fBkaridns.change_set\fR for the transaction (the audit log and the policies
of the API's role read them), and the serial is raised with
\fBkaridns_auth.bump_serial()\fR. SQLite3: the values go into the row of
\fBkaridns_audit_actor\fR and the version row. The id is 1 to 18 digits, the
change set 1 to 64 of letters, digits, \fB.\fR, \fB_\fR, \fB:\fR and \fB\-\fR.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-readback zone|changed\fR (apply)
\fBzone\fR (the default) reads the whole zone back through the loader's reader;
\fBchanged\fR only the rows this transaction wrote (the commit run of the API).
.TP 8
\fB\-\-version\-kind edit|template|import|rollback\fR, \fB\-\-rollback\-of N\fR, \fB\-\-comment TEXT\fR (apply)
The kind of the version row (\fBcreate\fR is chosen by \fB\-\-create\fR), the
version a rollback restores (needed with \fBrollback\fR, an error otherwise),
and the comment (at most 1024 octets).
.TP 8
\fB\-\-types\-list\fR
Print the record types a change file may name and exit.
.TP 8
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print the usage and exit with 0.
.TP 8
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version and the drivers this build has, and exit with 0.
.SH EXPORT OUTPUT
\fBexport <zone>\fR writes to standard output; \fBexport <zone> <file>\fR writes
the file (\fB\-\fR is standard output). \fB<zone>\fR is a name with or without
the final dot, in any case. Only primary zones (\fBNATIVE\fR, \fBMASTER\fR) have
records the server serves; a \fBSLAVE\fR, \fBCONSUMER\fR or \fBPRODUCER\fR zone
is \fBdb.export.zone\-type\fR. A zone with a row in \fBcryptokeys\fR but without
\fBPRESIGNED\fR is refused (the server does not serve it: online signing is not
supported).
.PP
The BIND text is \fB$ORIGIN\fR, \fB$TTL\fR (the TTL most records have), the tag
definitions, then one record per line: owners relative to the zone (\fB@\fR for
the apex), names inside the zone in RDATA relative, other names absolute with
the dot, TTL written when it differs from \fB$TTL\fR, strings quoted, long
base64 / hex blobs not wrapped. Every name, string and tag that comes from the
database is escaped, so no value can end its line, start a directive or a
comment or open a group; a record the format cannot hold makes the export fail
and names it (\fBrecord 12 (www.example.test. A): ...\fR).
.PP
\fBFiles.\fR A file is written to a temporary file in its directory and moved
into place: a zone that cannot be read leaves nothing, and a reader never sees
half a file. Without \fB\-\-force\fR the name is taken with \fBlink(2)\fR, so a
file that appeared in between is never replaced. Output files are never opened
through a symbolic link: an existing link is not followed (without
\fB\-\-force\fR it is an error, with \fB\-\-force\fR the link itself is
replaced). With \fB\-\-json\fR the zone text cannot go to standard output: give
a file.
.PP
\fB\fBexport \-\-all \-\-dir DIR\fB\fR writes \fB<name>.zone\fR (BIND format) or
\fB<name>.tinydns\fR (a tinydns data file) for every primary zone, in the order
of the names. The name is the zone name with every byte that is not a letter,
digit, \fB\-\fR, \fB_\fR or \fB.\fR (a dot as the first character included)
written as \fB%XX\fR, so a name never contains a path separator, starts with a
dot or contains a control character, and two zones never give the same file. A
zone that cannot be exported (the reader refuses its rows) is reported and the
others are written; the exit status is 1. With \fB\-\-view\fR only the zones of
the view are written.
.SS Export for the signer (\-\-signer)
\fBkaridns\-zone export \-\-signer <zone> <file>\fR writes the zone as the
\fBinput of \fBdnssec\-signzone\fB\fR, which is what \fBkaridns\-signer\fR
needs. The plain export is the zone as the server serves it, and the server
serves a signed zone only when its NSEC3 chain is complete: a zone that someone
changed after it was signed (a new name has no NSEC3 record and no signature
over it yet) is refused by the reader (\fBpresigned zone: no RRSIG(NSEC3) owner
for 'new.example.test.'\fR), and so by the plain export. With \fB\-\-signer\fR
the rows are read without the chain: the NSEC, NSEC3 and NSEC3PARAM records and
the metadata \fBPRESIGNED\fR and \fBNSEC3PARAM\fR are left out, so there is
nothing to rebuild or check. The RRSIG records (those over the chain as well,
since 0.6.1) and the DNSKEY, CDS and CDNSKEY records stay, so that
\fBdnssec\-signzone \-i \-u\fR keeps the signatures that are still good (an
RRset that changed has a signature that no longer verifies, and is signed again;
a chain record that changed or is new is signed, one that is gone loses its
signature) and makes the chain again; \fBimport \-\-signer\fR then writes only
the rows that differ. A signature over a chain record is exported only while its
owner is still a name of the zone (NSEC) or its hash is still the
\fBordername\fR of a row (NSEC3): the signature of a name that is gone would be
an orphan that the reader refuses. Sign this text with \fBdnssec\-signzone
\-u\fR (the signer does): without \fB\-u\fR the old chain is kept as it is and a
name that is new has no chain record. The text is the data of the database as it
is, signed and unsigned alike; the check "a zone with a row in \fBcryptokeys\fR
but without \fBPRESIGNED\fR is refused" is not made, because the metadata is not
read. On an unsigned zone \fB\-\-signer\fR gives the same text as the plain
export. It takes the options of \fBexport\fR (\fB\-\-all \-\-dir\fR as well) and
none of \fBimport\fR.
.SH JSON OUTPUT
\fBkaridns\-zone \-\-json <command> ...\fR writes the document of \fBkaricheck
\-\-json\fR with the first key \fB"karidns\-zone": 1\fR and \fB"command":
"import"\fR, \fB"export"\fR, \fB"apply"\fR, \fB"render"\fR or \fB"types"\fR;
\fBimport \-\-signer\fR adds the key \fBsigner\fR after \fBzones\fR (above),
\fBapply\fR the key \fBapply\fR (above), \fBrender\fR the key \fBrender\fR
(above), \fB\-\-types\-list\fR the key \fBtypes\fR; \fBmessages[]\fR,
\fBcounts\fR and \fBzones[]\fR are as there (\fBzones[]\fR has one entry per
zone imported or exported; \fBformat\fR is \fBbind\fR, \fBtinydns\fR, or
\fBdatabase\fR for a zone that failed before its format was known; \fBfile\fR is
the input or output file). Standard output carries only the document, standard
error nothing; the exit status is the one of the text mode. The codes are those
of \fBkaricheck(8)\fR (\fBusage.*\fR, \fBio.*\fR, \fBconf.*\fR, \fBzone.*\fR,
\fBdb.import.*\fR, \fBdb.export.*\fR, \fBdb.zone.*\fR; the list is in the manual
of \fBkaricheck\fR).
.SH AUDIT
A database of schema version 3 (\fBdb/karidns_ext.*.sql\fR) has an audit log:
\fBkaridns_auth.audit_log\fR on PostgreSQL, \fBkaridns_audit_log\fR on
SQLite3. It is empty and stays empty until the audit triggers are attached
(PostgreSQL: \fBSELECT karidns_auth.enable_audit('public')\fR, which the role
scripts of the WebAdmin do; SQLite3: apply
\fBdb/karidns_audit.sqlite3.sql\fR). With the triggers on, every row
\fBkaridns\-zone import\fR changes is logged: the table, the operation (\fBI\fR,
\fBU\fR, \fBD\fR), the zone, the row before and after as JSON, and who did it.
.PP
\fBWho.\fR PostgreSQL: the database role of the connection (\fBdb_role\fR), and
no principal. SQLite3: triggers can only read their own database, so the
importer writes one row to the table \fBkaridns_audit_actor\fR first in its
transaction and deletes it before the commit; the row says \fBkaridns\-zone:<OS
user>\fR. A database without that table (schema version 2) gets no row and the
import is as before. \fBimport\fR does not set \fBkaridns.principal\fR: a
session that does (the WebAdmin) is logged under that principal; \fBapply
\-\-principal P \-\-change\-set C\fR sets it (and, on SQLite3, writes it into
the actor row) and the audit rows of the change carry both.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-audit rows\fR (the default)
One audit row per changed record, tag, tag definition and metadata row. A
replace-import of a zone of a million records writes two million rows.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-audit summary\fR
PostgreSQL: each write transaction of the run begins with \fBSET LOCAL
karidns.audit = 'summary'\fR. The audit then writes one row for the transaction,
the table, the operation and the zone, which counts the statements and the rows
(\fB{"summary": true, "statements": 1000000, "rows": 1000000}\fR in
\fBafter\fR); the first statement inserts the row and the totals are written
into it when the transaction commits. The role of the WebAdmin
(\fBkaridns_api\fR) cannot turn the detail off, and neither can a login role
that is a member of it, also after \fBSET ROLE\fR to another role: the
restricted roles are the rows of the table
\fBkaridns_auth.audit_restricted_roles\fR (owner only; the role script of the
WebAdmin adds the role name it was given). The importer's role can, and so can
the signer's (\fBkaridns_signer\fR: a trusted daemon whose output is machine
made; \fBimport \-\-signer\fR uses summary mode by default). SQLite3: the audit
has rows only; the option is accepted and the actor row is written as with
\fBrows\fR.
.PP
\fBZone names in the log.\fR Every audit row of a zone's data names the zone
(\fBzone_name\fR), also the rows that a zone delete removes through foreign keys
(records, metadata, tag definitions, views). The one exception: the rows of
\fBkaridns_record_tags\fR and \fBkaridns_record_tinydns\fR that go with their
record (when the record or its zone is deleted) have \fBzone_name\fR NULL,
because the record is gone when they are deleted. The \fBD\fR row of the record
carries the zone, and its \fBrow_id\fR is the \fBrecord_id\fR of those
rows. (PostgreSQL remembers the name of a zone that is being deleted in a
transaction-local setting \fBkaridns.zc_<hash of the schema>_<domain id>\fR; it
ends with the transaction.)
.PP
\fBRow level security.\fR When the role script of the WebAdmin or of the signer
has switched row level security on (PostgreSQL), the importer's role must be a
member of \fBkaridns_rls_trusted\fR, or it sees no
rows. \fBdb/karidns_writer_role.pgsql.sql\fR makes its role a member, whichever
script ran first. See THE AUTH SCHEMA, ROLES AND THE AUDIT LOG in
\fBkaridns\-dbloader(8)\fR.
.PP
\fBCost.\fR A replace-import of 100,000 records into PostgreSQL 18 (one
\fBINSERT\fR per record, one transaction) took 17 s without the audit, 36 s with
\fB\-\-audit rows\fR and 36 s with \fB\-\-audit summary\fR. Summary mode saves
space in the log, not time.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP 8
\fB0\fR
Every zone was imported or exported (warnings do not change this).
.TP 8
\fB1\fR
Something failed: an error of the zone checks, a serial that does not grow, a
zone the loader would refuse, the database, a configuration that cannot be read,
an output file. Nothing of a failed zone was written to the database.
.TP 8
\fB2\fR
Usage error (an unknown option or command, a missing value, an option of the
other command, a wrong number of arguments, \fB\-\-json\fR with an export to
standard output, \fB\-\-signer\fR with \fB\-\-mode\fR, \fB\-\-types\fR,
\fB\-\-serial\fR, \fB\-\-view\fR, \fB\-\-type\fR or \fB\-\-all\fR). The usage
goes to standard error (the message only, in the document, with \fB\-\-json\fR);
nothing was done.
.TP 8
\fB3\fR
\fBimport \-\-signer\fR: the zone in the database is no longer the zone the
signed file was made from (a record or the SOA differs, or the serial does not
grow, or another session changed the zone while the tool wrote it). Nothing was
written (\fBdb.import.changed\fR). Sign the zone again. \fBapply\fR: the zone is
not at the generation \fB\-\-expect\-generation\fR named, or a session that
committed after the snapshot of the transaction changed a row it wrote. Nothing
was written; check the change again.
.TP 8
\fB4\fR
\fBimport \-\-signer\fR, \fBapply\fR: the zone is locked by another import, or
the database is busy for longer than the tool waits. Nothing was written
(\fBdb.import.busy\fR). Try again.
.SH THE WRITER ROLE (PostgreSQL)
\fBimport\fR writes \fBdomains\fR, \fBrecords\fR, \fBdomainmetadata\fR and the
\fBkaridns_*\fR zone tables; \fBexport\fR only
reads. \fBdb/karidns_writer_role.pgsql.sql\fR gives a role exactly that and no
more (the database packages install it in \fB/usr/local/share/karidns/db/\fR,
next to \fBkaridns_loader_role.pgsql.sql\fR, which the server's loader uses; do
not give the loader this role):
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
psql \-X \-v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 \-c "CREATE ROLE karidns_writer LOGIN"
psql \-X \-v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 \-v role=karidns_writer \-v schema=public \-f /usr/local/share/karidns/db/karidns_writer_role.pgsql.sql karidns
.fi
.RE
.PP
(\fBrole\fR is the importer's role, \fBschema\fR the schema of the tables; run
it once per schema when views have schemas of their own; it can be run
again.) The role gets \fBSELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE\fR on \fBdomains\fR,
\fBrecords\fR and \fBdomainmetadata\fR; \fBSELECT\fR on every \fBkaridns_*\fR
table and \fBINSERT, UPDATE, DELETE\fR on the zone tables
(\fBkaridns_domain_views\fR, \fBkaridns_record_tags\fR,
\fBkaridns_record_tinydns\fR, \fBkaridns_zone_tagdefs\fR); what the change log
and the reload requests need (\fBINSERT, DELETE\fR on \fBkaridns_changes\fR and
\fBkaridns_reload_requests\fR, because the triggers run with the rights of the
writer); \fBUSAGE\fR on the sequences. For \fBcryptokeys\fR all rights are
revoked and only the \fBcolumn\fR privilege \fBSELECT (domain_id)\fR is granted
(the importer asks whether a zone has keys, to warn that unsigned data makes the
loader refuse a zone with keys): \fBthe role can never read
\fBcryptokeys.content\fB\fR, the private keys. Authentication is yours: a UNIX
socket with peer authentication for the user who runs the tool is the simplest;
put the connection string in a file of mode 0600:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
# /usr/local/etc/karidns/writer.conninfo
host=/var/run/postgresql dbname=karidns user=karidns_writer
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBkaridns\-zone import \-\-conninfo\-file
/usr/local/etc/karidns/writer.conninfo example.test
example.test.zone\fR. SQLite3 has no roles: the user running the tool needs read
and write access to the database file and its directory.
.SS The signer role
\fBimport \-\-signer\fR needs less: \fBdb/karidns_signer_role.pgsql.sql\fR (see
\fBkaridns\-dbloader(8)\fR, "Roles and row level security") gives the signer's
role \fBSELECT\fR on everything, \fBINSERT\fR and \fBDELETE\fR on the DNSSEC
records, \fBUPDATE\fR of records (a trigger lets it change only \fBordername\fR
and \fBauth\fR of a record that is not a DNSSEC record, and of an SOA record
only the serial besides), and the three metadata kinds it owns. The tool deletes
the DNSSEC records by id and never touches the tag tables, so that role is
enough, with row level security on.
.SH EXAMPLES
Import a zone file into the database of the configuration, then check what was
written:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
karidns\-zone import \-c /usr/local/etc/karidns/karidns.conf example.test /var/zones/example.test.zone
karicheck db
.fi
.RE
.PP
See what a merge would change without writing it; take the data from standard
input:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
karidns\-zone import \-\-sqlite3 /var/db/karidns/pdns.sqlite3 \-\-dry\-run \-\-mode merge example.test \- < new\-records.zone
.fi
.RE
.PP
Import every zone of a tinydns data file (each in its own transaction), then
export one back:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
karidns\-zone import \-\-all \-\-sqlite3 /var/db/karidns/pdns.sqlite3 /var/tinydns/root/data
karidns\-zone export \-c /usr/local/etc/karidns/karidns.conf example.test > example.test.zone
.fi
.RE
.PP
Write a zone that \fBdnssec\-signzone\fR signed from an export of it (what
\fBkaridns\-signer\fR does; the exit status is 3 when the zone changed
meanwhile):
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
karidns\-zone export \-\-signer \-c karidns.conf example.test example.test.in
dnssec\-signzone \-o example.test \-f example.test.signed \-N increment \-3 \- \-H 0 \-K keys example.test.in Kexample.test.+013+11111 Kexample.test.+013+22222
karidns\-zone import \-\-signer \-\-json \-c karidns.conf example.test example.test.signed
.fi
.RE
.PP
See a stored MX record as a change set takes it (the priority in front, every
name absolute):
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
printf 'mail.example.test\etMX\et10\etmail2.example.test\en' | karidns\-zone render \-\-json example.test
.fi
.RE
.PP
Take the serial from the database (a changed file whose serial was not raised),
or restore a backup as it was:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
karidns\-zone import \-\-serial increment \-c karidns.conf example.test example.test.zone
karidns\-zone import \-\-serial keep      \-c karidns.conf example.test /var/backups/zones/example.test.zone
.fi
.RE
.SS Backups with export \-\-all
.RS 2
.nf
karidns\-zone export \-\-all \-\-dir /var/backups/zones/$(date +%Y%m%d) \-c /usr/local/etc/karidns/karidns.conf
.fi
.RE
.PP
writes one file per zone (\fBexample.test.zone\fR,
\fBlan.example.tinydns\fR, ...). The files are canonical, so a \fBdiff \-r\fR of
two backups shows what changed; a restore is \fBkaridns\-zone import \-\-serial
keep\fR of the files. The round trip is exact: file -> import -> export ->
import -> export gives the same bytes both times, for signed, tagged, tinydns,
\fB$GENERATE\fR and \fB$INCLUDE\fR zones (\fBtests/run_karidns_zone_test.sh\fR).
.SS When the database is down: export, then a file zone
The loader needs the database; a file zone does not. Keep a recent export and
serve it from a file when the database is gone (D-PRIO: a file zone in
\fBkaridns.conf\fR has priority over the database zone of the same name, so the
server serves the file as soon as it is configured):
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
karidns\-zone export \-c karidns.conf example.test /usr/local/etc/karidns/zones/example.test.zone
.fi
.RE
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
zone "example.test" { type master; file "/usr/local/etc/karidns/zones/example.test.zone"; };
.fi
.RE
.PP
then \fBkarictl reconfig\fR. A signed zone is exported with its signatures and
the NSEC3 records, so the file serves it as the database did. Remove the
\fBzone\fR statement when the database is back (\fBkaridns\-zone import
\-\-serial increment\fR first if the file was changed).
.SH LIMITS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBimport\fR reads up to 64 MiB from standard input. A zone file read from
standard input cannot use \fB$INCLUDE\fR (there is no directory to read from;
\fBzone.include\-stdin\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
One zone per \fBimport <zone> <file>\fR: a BIND-format file with several zones
is not split. \fBimport \-\-all\fR reads tinydns data files only.
.IP \(bu 2
The size limits are those of the configuration (\fBmax\-records\fR, ...) and of
the database block (\fBmax\-zone\-records\fR, \fBmax\-zone\-bytes\fR); the
loader reads at most \fBmax\-zone\-records\fR records of a zone.
.IP \(bu 2
What does not carry over from a tinydns data file: the SOA serial a \fB.\fR line
takes from the data file's modification time is stored as the number the
importer saw (use a \fBZ\fR line, or \fB\-\-serial\fR), and the text of the data
file (comments, order, \fB\-\fR lines) is not kept.
.IP \(bu 2
Two importers of one zone wait for each other (a transaction-level advisory lock
on PostgreSQL, \fBBEGIN IMMEDIATE\fR on SQLite3) and give up after the busy
timeout with \fBdb.import.busy\fR; try again.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBexport\fR of a zone with more records than \fBmax\-zone\-records\fR (default
10,000,000 for the reader) fails like the loader does.
.SH FILES
.TP 8
\fB/usr/local/share/karidns/db/karidns_check_role.pgsql.sql\fR
The privileges of the check role of the API (the writer role's, membership of
\fBkaridns_rls_trusted\fR and \fBkaridns_never_commit\fR, the never-commit
triggers; database packages).
.TP 8
\fB/usr/local/etc/karidns/karidns.conf\fR
The configuration read when \fB\-c\fR is not given.
.TP 8
\fB/usr/local/share/karidns/db/karidns_writer_role.pgsql.sql\fR
The privileges of the PostgreSQL role that imports (database packages; \fBdb/\fR
in a source tree).
.TP 8
\fB/usr/local/bin/karidns\-zone\fR
The tool (database packages; the SQLite3 / PostgreSQL drivers of the package are
linked in: \fBkaridns\-zone \-\-version\fR lists them).
